\lispPackageChapter{livermore/stocks}

The \texttt{livermore/stocks} package is a price table: daily bars loaded from
Yahoo-style \textsc{Csv} files, optional monthly and yearly aggregates, and a
set of moving-average and directional signals.  The working series is
\texttt{(records \lispVar{table})}, which applies \texttt{preferred-records}
and then clips to \texttt{records-start} and \texttt{records-end}.

\section{Parameters}

\lispParameterSubsection{*initial-money*}

The default cash stake for the analyzers.  Default $100000.00$.

\lispParameterSubsection{*livermore-data-directory*}

The directory of Yahoo \textsc{Csv} files, under the \textsc{Asdf} system
source directory.

\section{Classes}

\lispClassSubsection{table-record}

One \textsc{Ohlc} bar.

\begin{description}
\item[\texttt{opening-time}, \texttt{closing-time}] Universal times.
\item[\texttt{descriptive-time}] A printable date.
\item[\texttt{opening-price}, \texttt{high-price}, \texttt{low-price},
  \texttt{closing-price}] Unadjusted prices.
\item[\texttt{trading-volume}] Session volume.
\item[\texttt{adjusted-closing-price}] Split-adjusted close.
\end{description}

\texttt{adjustment} is adjusted close over close.
\texttt{adjusted-opening-price}, \texttt{adjusted-high-price}, and
\texttt{adjusted-low-price} multiply the unadjusted prices by that factor.
\texttt{table-record} is also a constructor.

\lispClassSubsection{table}

A price series for one ticker.

\begin{description}
\item[\texttt{ticker-symbol}] Canonical ticker.
\item[\texttt{preferred-records}] A function of the table that returns the
  working bars, default \texttt{\#'daily-records}.
\item[\texttt{records-start}, \texttt{records-end}] The slice of that series.
\end{description}

\texttt{daily-records} is the raw daily series.
\texttt{monthly-records} and \texttt{yearly-records} build \textsc{Ohlc}
aggregates on first use.

On a table, \texttt{opening-time}, \texttt{opening-price}, and the other
price accessors map over \texttt{records} and return a list.

\section{Loading}

\lispFunctionSubsection{canonical-ticker}

Uppercase ticker string, following Yahoo's style.

\lispFunctionSubsection{table-filename}

The path of the local \texttt{\textit{TICKER}-table.csv}.

\lispFunctionSubsection{load-table}

Also called \texttt{load-table-from-yahoo}.  Reads that file, skips the
header row, and sorts the bars by opening time.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(load-table \lispVar{ticker-symbol} \&key preferred-records)}

\texttt{retrieve-stock-data} fetches a Yahoo \texttt{table.csv} with
\texttt{wget}.  \texttt{retrieve-stocks-data} does so for every ticker in
\texttt{*ticker-descriptions*}.

\section{Changes and Statistics}

\texttt{difference}, \texttt{value-ratio}, and \texttt{percent-change}
compare a key at \texttt{start} and \texttt{end}.
\texttt{percent-change} is $100$ times the value ratio.
\texttt{spread-difference} and its siblings compare
\texttt{position+spread} to \texttt{position}.

The sample statistics from \texttt{livermore/statistics} are specialized on
\texttt{table}.  \texttt{sample-z-score} and
\texttt{unbiased-sample-z-score} are the $z$-score of the key at
\texttt{start}.

\texttt{shares-buyable} is how many whole shares a sum of money buys at the
adjusted close.  \texttt{buy-and-hold} grows an initial sum by the value
ratio.

\section{Indicators}

\texttt{simple-moving-average} (\texttt{sma}) is the unweighted mean of each
window.  \texttt{arithmetically-weighted-moving-average} (\texttt{awma})
weights the newest point most.  \texttt{exponential-moving-average}
(\texttt{ema}) uses $\alpha = 2/(n+1)$.

\texttt{moving-average-convergence-divergence} (\texttt{macd}) is the
difference of two \textsc{Ema}s.  \texttt{macd-signal} is an \textsc{Ema} of
that difference.

\texttt{upward-changes} and \texttt{downward-changes} are the positive
day-to-day moves, else $0$.  \texttt{relative-strength} is the ratio of a
moving average of those two series.  \texttt{ema-rs} is Wilder's form;
\texttt{ema-rs-27} is the textbook period.

\texttt{simple-directional-signal} is true where the price \lispVar{period}
steps later is higher.  The matching \texttt{*-gains} and
\texttt{*-performance} functions turn a signal into a product of day-to-day
returns, holding cash when the signal is false.

\texttt{sma-crossover-signal} is true when the short \textsc{Sma} exceeds
\lispVar{ratio} times the long \textsc{Sma}.
\texttt{directional-sma-crossover-signal} returns \texttt{:long},
\texttt{:short}, or \texttt{:cash} from those averages and the slope of the
long average.  The \texttt{*-optimal} functions search period and ratio.
